-## Facts & Assumptions
-
-**Given:** Positive integers $m,n$, $A\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}$, $y\in\mathbb R^m$, $\epsilon\ge0$, step sizes and caps satisfying the Algorithm's checks, and the supplied deterministic finite proximal solvers and sound checkers.
-
-[A1] The solver/checker programs have the termination and certificate-soundness properties specified in the Algorithm and in the primal-dual hybrid-gradient interface; no existence of a finite evaluator for an arbitrary proximal map is assumed.
-
-[F1] [[lem-ip-bpdn-objectives-proper-closed-convex]]: $f(x)=\|x\|_1$ and the residual-ball indicator $g$ are proper, closed, and convex for these inputs.
-
-[F2] [[def-proper-closed-convex-function-on-rn]]: the primal and dual functions must be proper, closed, and convex.
-
-[F3] [[def-fenchel-conjugate-on-finite-dimensional-euclidean-space]] and [[lem-fenchel-conjugate-is-proper-closed-convex]]: $g^*(p)=\sup_u(\langle p,u\rangle-g(u))$ is proper, closed, and convex.
-
-[F4] [[def-induced-matrix-p-norm]]: $\|A\|_2=\sup_{\|x\|_2\le1}\|Ax\|_2$.
-
-[F5] [[alg-pdhg-primal-dual-hybrid-gradient]]: for step sizes $\alpha,\beta>0$ with $\alpha\beta\|A\|_2^2<1$, the recurrence is $p^{k+1}=\operatorname{prox}_{\beta g^*}(p^k+\beta A\bar x^k)$, $x^{k+1}=\operatorname{prox}_{\alpha f}(x^k-\alpha A^*p^{k+1})$, and $\bar x^{k+1}=2x^{k+1}-x^k$; its supplied checkers certify exact proximal minimizers, with explicit failure and cap-exhausted statuses.
-
-[F6] [[lem-ip-bpdn-minimizer-exists]]: if the residual ball is nonempty, the objective $\|z\|_1$ attains its minimum there.
-
-[F7] [[lem-ip-bpdn-dual-certificates]]: a feasible $x,p$ with $\|A^*p\|_\infty\le1$ and $\|x\|_1=-\langle p,y\rangle-\epsilon\|p\|_2$ certifies global optimality; a nonzero $p$ with $A^*p=0$ and $|\langle p,y\rangle|>\epsilon\|p\|_2$ certifies infeasibility.
-
-[F8] [[def-p-norms-on-rn]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[thm-adjoint-exists-and-is-unique-in-finite-dimension]], and [[thm-proximal-operator-existence-resolvent-firm-nonexpansive]]: the norms, pairings, adjoint, and proximal minimizers use the standard finite-dimensional real Euclidean structures.
-
-[F9] [[def-proximal-mapping]]: $\operatorname{prox}_{tq}(v)$ is the set of global minimizers of $q(w)+\|w-v\|_2^2/(2t)$.
