-## Verification
-
-1.1 By [F1] and [F2], $f$ and $g$ meet the primal proper-closed-convex preconditions. If the residual ball is nonempty, [F6] also guarantees that the target minimum is attained; this does not imply that the finite procedure finds a certificate. By [F3], their dual conjugate input $g^*$ also meets them. The solver/checker pairs are assumed to meet exactly [A1], so every accepted proximal update is a true minimizer of its stated subproblem. [given, A1, F1, F2, F3, F6, F8]
-
-1.2 The exact data and step-size check place the update in the regime of [F5]; the displayed assignments are its recurrence with $K=A$. The finite wrapper propagates each proximal failure or exhaustion and only examines a state after all requested sweeps complete. If all tests fail, returning budget-exhausted states only that this capped run found no supplied certificate; it asserts neither infeasibility nor convergence. [given, A1, F4, F5, F9, construct]
-
-2.1 If the first certificate test passes, [F7] proves that no vector is feasible, so **infeasible** is correct. If the second test passes, [F7] proves that the returned feasible vector minimizes the objective, so **success** is correct. The tests are disjoint because an infeasibility certificate says the residual ball contains no image $Az$, whereas a success certificate supplies one; therefore the stated priority cannot return two incompatible conclusions. [F7, given, algebra] ∎